<h2>Understanding the Summary Cards</h2>

<p>
    The top section shows three key metrics:
</p>

<h3>Active Policies (12 months)</h3>
<ul>
    <li>Counts all policies that were active at any time during the last 12 months</li>
    <li>Includes policies that started, ended, or were active throughout the period</li>
    <li>Provides a snapshot of your active book</li>
</ul>

<h3>Total Premium</h3>
<ul>
    <li>Sum of all premium amounts for active policies</li>
    <li>Formatted as currency for easy reading</li>
    <li>Represents the total premium volume in your book</li>
</ul>

<h3>Average Premium per Policy</h3>
<ul>
    <li>Calculated by dividing total premium by the number of active policies</li>
    <li>Helps you understand the average policy size</li>
    <li>Useful for benchmarking and trend analysis</li>
</ul>

<!-- IMAGE PLACEHOLDER: Screenshot of the summary cards section with callouts explaining each metric. Show the formatted currency values and the calculation method. -->

<h2>Client Breakdown Table</h2>

<p>
    The data grid shows detailed information for each client:
</p>

<ul>
    <li><strong>Client name</strong> – Name of the client</li>
    <li><strong>Lookup code</strong> – Client identifier code</li>
    <li><strong>Active policies</strong> – Number of active policies for that client</li>
    <li><strong>Total premium</strong> – Sum of premiums for that client's policies</li>
    <li><strong>Average premium</strong> – Average premium per policy for that client</li>
</ul>

<p>
    The table is sortable and can be filtered, making it easy to:
</p>

<ul>
    <li>Identify your largest clients by premium</li>
    <li>Find clients with the most policies</li>
    <li>Compare average premiums across clients</li>
    <li>Spot trends or outliers</li>
</ul>

